\title{Window Saturation, the Matched Filter,\\ and the Interior-Argmax Certificate}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-08-26}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A widespread empirical practice scores a family of predictors by aggregating
them into a single \emph{window statistic} $S_{w,B} = \sum_{i<B} w_i v_i$,
regressing a response $y$ on it, and reading off the cutoff $B^\star$ at which
the coefficient of determination $R^2$ is maximised --- the ``saturation
location''. We develop the exact theory of this curve for pairwise orthogonal
columns and show that the standard interpretation of $B^\star$ is untenable.

We prove: (i) a residual decomposition identifying the window score with the
ordinary-least-squares $R^2$ and simultaneously yielding OLS optimality and
$0 \le R^2 \le 1$; (ii) an exact one-step law computing
$R^2(w,B{+}1) - R^2(w,B)$ in closed form, with an iff criterion for a cutoff
step to help, whose corollaries are that a response-orthogonal column with
nonzero weight strictly \emph{dilutes} the score while a column at least as
efficient as the running slope strictly raises it; (iii) a saturation theorem
--- a matched signal block followed by pure noise produces a strictly unimodal
curve with a \emph{unique interior} argmax; (iv) matched-filter dominance: the
weight $w_i = a_i/s_i$ maximises the score at every cutoff simultaneously, its
curve is monotone in $B$, and $E(m)/\|y\|^2$ with $E(B)=\sum_{i<B}a_i^2/s_i$ is
a global cap on the entire instrument, attained; (v) the
\emph{interior-argmax certificate}: a strict interior maximum of a measured
curve proves the weight in use is not the matched filter, up to scale; (vi) a
peak-margin identity showing the drop from an interior peak to the far cutoff
equals the peak score times the relative added mass, together with a sharp
$\delta/2$ stability threshold for the argmax under perturbations; (vii) a
realizability theorem --- within \emph{any} fixed orthogonal column family,
\emph{every} interior location $1 \le t < m$ is the unique argmax for a suitable
response, so $B^\star$ carries no information about the columns; and (viii) a
refutation of unimodality: even for orthonormal columns with unit weights and
decreasing per-column efficiencies, the prefix curve can have a strict interior
local minimum and hence two local maxima.

Pairwise balanced $\pm1$ designs of strength two (Hadamard rows being the model
case) supply window models automatically, and the order-$4$ Hadamard design
furnishes an explicit instance with curve $0,\tfrac12,1,\tfrac23,\tfrac12$ and
peak margin $\tfrac12$. The practical upshot reverses the usual reading: an
interior saturation peak is a diagnostic of weight mismatch, not a measurement
of where information in the predictor family runs out.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical instrument}

Consider an experiment producing one observation per sampled modulus $N$: a
response $y \in \R^n$ recording, say, a log hit-rate. Explanatory information is
carried by per-prime indicator columns $v_\ell \in \R^n$, one for each odd prime
$\ell$. Rather than fitting a multiple regression on hundreds of columns, the
standard instrument compresses them into a single weighted aggregate
\[
  S_{w,B} \;=\; \sum_{\ell \le B} w(\ell)\, v_\ell ,
\]
regresses $y$ on this one predictor, and records $R^2(w,B)$ as the cutoff $B$
runs along a factor-of-two grid. Two knobs exist: the weight $w$ and the cutoff
$B$.

A concrete instance, on $n=128$ samples with odd primes $3 \le \ell \le 1600$
and the weight $w(\ell)=\ell^{-1/2}$, produced the following.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$B$ & $R^2$, $\ell^{-1/2}$ & $R^2$, $\ell^{-1}$ & $\Delta R^2$\\
\midrule
$100$  & $.5279$ & $.4388$ & $+0.0891$\\
$200$  & $.5976$ & $.4621$ & $+0.1355$\\
$\mathbf{400}$  & $\mathbf{.6242}$ & $.4731$ & $\mathbf{+0.1511}$\\
$800$  & $.5913$ & $.4748$ & $+0.1165$\\
$1600$ & $.6137$ & $.4795$ & $+0.1342$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Three features invite interpretation. (a) The $\ell^{-1/2}$ curve has a unique
\emph{interior} maximum at $B=400$. (b) It dominates the $\ell^{-1}$ curve at
\emph{every} cutoff, with no apparent weight-by-cutoff interaction. (c) The
$\ell^{-1}$ curve, recomputed, is a flat plateau above $B=200$ whose nominal
maximum sits at the \emph{edge}, $B=1600$, exceeding its value at $400$ by
$0.006$ --- noise level. A bootstrap over $500$ resamples split the argmax as
$\{400\!:\!276,\ 1600\!:\!178,\ 200\!:\!37,\ 800\!:\!9\}$, the runner-up trailing
the peak by only $0.0105$.

The customary conclusion --- ``arithmetic information saturates at
$B^\star=400$'' --- treats the peak location as a property of the prime columns.
This paper shows that reading is wrong in a strong and precise sense, and
replaces it with correct statements.

\subsection{Notation}

Throughout, $\ip{u}{v}=\sum_{j=1}^n u_jv_j$ is the Euclidean inner product on
$\R^n$ and $\|u\|^2 = \ip{u}{u}$. Sums $\sum_{i<B}$ run over $i=0,\dots,B-1$;
empty sums are $0$.

\section{The window model}

\begin{definition}[Window model]
A \emph{window model} of size $(n,m)$ consists of columns
$v_0,\dots,v_{m-1}\in\R^n$ and a response $y\in\R^n$ such that
(1) $\|v_i\|^2>0$ for $i<m$; (2) $\ip{v_i}{v_j}=0$ for $i\ne j$, $i,j<m$;
(3) $\|y\|^2>0$. We write $s_i=\|v_i\|^2$ (the \emph{mass} of column $i$) and
$a_i=\ip{v_i}{y}$ (its \emph{signal}).
\end{definition}

Condition (2) is the idealisation ``distinct primes contribute independent
information''.

\begin{definition}[Window statistic and score]
For a weight $w:\mathbb{N}\to\R$ and a cutoff $B\le m$ set
\[
  S_{w,B}=\sum_{i<B}w_iv_i,\qquad A_B=\sum_{i<B}w_ia_i,\qquad
  \Sigma_B=\sum_{i<B}w_i^2s_i,
\]
and define the \emph{window score}
$R^2(w,B) = A_B^2/(\Sigma_B\|y\|^2)$, with the convention $0/0=0$, so
$R^2(w,0)=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:scalars}
For every $B\le m$, $\ip{S_{w,B}}{y}=A_B$ and $\|S_{w,B}\|^2=\Sigma_B$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Expand and exchange the order of summation. For the second identity each cross
term $w_iw_k\ip{v_i}{v_k}$ with $i\ne k$ vanishes by orthogonality, leaving the
diagonal $\sum_{i<B}w_i^2s_i$.
\end{proof}

Thus $R^2(w,B)$ is the squared cosine between $S_{w,B}$ and $y$.

\begin{theorem}[Residual decomposition]\label{thm:rss}
Let $x,y\in\R^n$ with $\|x\|^2>0$, $\|y\|^2>0$, and write
$\mathrm{Rsq}(x,y)=\ip{x}{y}^2/(\|x\|^2\|y\|^2)$. Then for every $b\in\R$,
\[
  \|y-bx\|^2 = \|y\|^2\bigl(1-\mathrm{Rsq}(x,y)\bigr)
   + \|x\|^2\Bigl(b-\frac{\ip{x}{y}}{\|x\|^2}\Bigr)^{\!2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\|y-bx\|^2=\|y\|^2-2b\ip{x}{y}+b^2\|x\|^2$ and complete the square in
$b$; the constant term is $\|y\|^2-\ip{x}{y}^2/\|x\|^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[OLS optimality and calibration]\label{cor:ols}
(i) $\|y-bx\|^2\ge\|y\|^2(1-\mathrm{Rsq}(x,y))$ for every $b$, with equality
exactly at the OLS slope $b=\ip{x}{y}/\|x\|^2$.
(ii) $0\le \mathrm{Rsq}(x,y)\le 1$, hence $0\le R^2(w,B)\le 1$ for all weights
and all $B\le m$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
(i) The second term of Theorem~\ref{thm:rss} is a nonnegative square vanishing
precisely at the OLS slope. (ii) Nonnegativity is clear; evaluating
Theorem~\ref{thm:rss} at the OLS slope makes the left-hand side a sum of squares,
so $\|y\|^2(1-\mathrm{Rsq})\ge 0$. Degenerate cases follow from the convention.
\end{proof}

\section{The step calculus}

Fix a weight $w$ and a cutoff $B<m$, and write
$p=w_Ba_B$ (signal added) and $c=w_B^2s_B$ (mass added), so $A_{B+1}=A_B+p$ and
$\Sigma_{B+1}=\Sigma_B+c$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact step law]\label{thm:step}
If $\Sigma_B>0$ then
\[
  R^2(w,B{+}1)-R^2(w,B)
  =\frac{\Sigma_B\,p\,(2A_B+p)-A_B^2c}{\Sigma_B(\Sigma_B+c)\|y\|^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Clearing denominators reduces the claim to the polynomial identity
$(A+p)^2\Sigma-A^2(\Sigma+c)=\Sigma p(2A+p)-A^2c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Step criterion]\label{cor:stepiff}
If $\Sigma_B>0$, then $R^2(w,B)<R^2(w,B{+}1)$ if and only if
$A_B^2 c<\Sigma_B\,p\,(2A_B+p)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Noise dilutes]\label{thm:dilute}
Let $B<m$ with $\Sigma_B>0$ and $A_B\ne0$. If $a_B=0$ and $w_B\ne0$ then
$R^2(w,B{+}1)<R^2(w,B)$ strictly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Then $p=0$ and $c=w_B^2s_B>0$, so the numerator in Theorem~\ref{thm:step} is
$-A_B^2c<0$ while the denominator is positive.
\end{proof}

Saturation is thus not asymptotic flattening but strict dilution: under a fixed
weight, admitting a signal-free column destroys score, because it inflates
$\|S_{w,B}\|$ without moving $\ip{S_{w,B}}{y}$.

\begin{theorem}[Signal helps]\label{thm:help}
Let $B<m$ with $\Sigma_B>0$ and $A_B>0$. If $p>0$ and $p\Sigma_B\ge A_Bc$ then
$R^2(w,B)<R^2(w,B{+}1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiplying $p\Sigma_B\ge A_Bc$ by $A_B>0$ gives $A_Bp\Sigma_B\ge A_B^2c$; adding
the strictly positive $\Sigma_Bp(A_B+p)$ yields
$\Sigma_Bp(2A_B+p)>A_B^2c$, which is Corollary~\ref{cor:stepiff}.
\end{proof}

The hypothesis says the new column's efficiency $p/c$ is at least the running
slope $A_B/\Sigma_B$.

\section{The saturation theorem}

\begin{definition}[Matched signal block]
A weight $w$ is \emph{matched on the block} $[0,t)$ with slope $\rho$ if
$w_ia_i=\rho\,w_i^2s_i$ for all $i<t$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:rho}
If $w$ is matched on $[0,t)$ with slope $\rho$ then $A_B=\rho\Sigma_B$ for all
$B\le t$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $B$; the step adds $w_Ba_B=\rho w_B^2s_B$ to both sides.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation: unique interior argmax]\label{thm:sat}
Let $M$ be a window model, $1\le t\le m$, and $w$ a weight with
\begin{itemize}
\item $w_ia_i=\rho\,w_i^2s_i$ and $w_ia_i>0$ for all $i<t$;
\item $a_i=0$ for all $t\le i<m$;
\item $w_i\ne0$ for all $t\le i<m$.
\end{itemize}
Then $B\mapsto R^2(w,B)$ is strictly increasing on $\{0,\dots,t\}$ and strictly
decreasing on $\{t,\dots,m\}$; consequently $R^2(w,B)<R^2(w,t)$ for every
$B\le m$ with $B\ne t$, so the argmax set is exactly $\{t\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Positivity on the block gives $A_B>0$, $\Sigma_B>0$ for $1\le B\le t$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:rho} the running slope is constantly $\rho$, so each block column
satisfies $p\Sigma_B=A_Bc$ with $p>0$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:help} applies: strict
increase at every step below $t$ (from the empty window the score jumps from $0$
to a positive value). For $B\ge t$ the numerator is frozen while each step adds
positive mass, so Theorem~\ref{thm:dilute} gives strict decrease. Chaining the
strict one-step inequalities gives the two monotone branches.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}\label{ex:sat}
Take $n=m=4$, columns the standard basis $e_0,\dots,e_3$ of $\R^4$, and
$y=(1,1,0,0)$; then $s_i=1$, $a=(1,1,0,0)$, $\|y\|^2=2$. With unit weights the
hypotheses hold with $\rho=1$, $t=2$, and the curve is
$0,\tfrac12,1,\tfrac23,\tfrac12$.
\end{example}

\section{The matched filter and the interior-argmax certificate}

\begin{definition}[Matched filter; explained signal]
The \emph{matched filter} is $w^{\mf}_i=a_i/s_i$, the per-column
single-predictor regression slope. The \emph{explained signal} is
$E(B)=\sum_{i<B}a_i^2/s_i$ for $B\le m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:mf}
For $B\le m$ the matched filter has $A_B=\Sigma_B=E(B)$, hence
$R^2(w^{\mf},B)=E(B)/\|y\|^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$w^{\mf}_ia_i=a_i^2/s_i$ and $(w^{\mf}_i)^2s_i=a_i^2/s_i$; substitute and
cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No interior maximum for the matched filter]\label{thm:mono}
$B\mapsto R^2(w^{\mf},B)$ is nondecreasing on $\{0,\dots,m\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$E$ is a sum of nonnegative terms; divide by $\|y\|^2>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Matched-filter dominance]\label{thm:dom}
For every weight $w$ and every cutoff $B\le m$,
$R^2(w,B)\le R^2(w^{\mf},B)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\Sigma_B=0$ then every used weight annihilates its column's mass, so $A_B=0$
and the left side is $0$. Otherwise put $u_i=w_i\sqrt{s_i}$ and
$g_i=a_i/\sqrt{s_i}$ for $i<B$, so $A_B=\sum u_ig_i$, $\Sigma_B=\sum u_i^2$,
$E(B)=\sum g_i^2$. Cauchy--Schwarz gives $A_B^2\le\Sigma_BE(B)$; divide by
$\Sigma_B\|y\|^2$.
\end{proof}

Dominance holds at \emph{every} cutoff simultaneously: a better weight shifts the
whole curve up rather than trading one region for another. This is the exact form
of the empirical ``plateau raised everywhere''.

\begin{theorem}[Global cap, attained]\label{thm:cap}
For every weight $w$ and every $B\le m$, $R^2(w,B)\le E(m)/\|y\|^2$, and
$R^2(w^{\mf},m)=E(m)/\|y\|^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:dom} with monotonicity of $E$; attainment is
Lemma~\ref{lem:mf} at $B=m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Scale invariance]\label{lem:scale}
For $c\ne0$, $R^2(cw,B)=R^2(w,B)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Numerator and denominator both scale by $c^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Interior-argmax certificate]\label{thm:cert}
Suppose $R^2(w,m)<R^2(w,t)$ for some $t<m$. Then there is no constant $c\ne0$
with $w_i=c\,w^{\mf}_i$ for all $i<m$: the weight in use is provably not the
matched filter, not even up to global rescaling.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $w=c\,w^{\mf}$ on $[0,m)$ then by Lemma~\ref{lem:scale}, and since the score
at cutoff $B$ depends only on the weight below $B$, we get
$R^2(w,B)=R^2(w^{\mf},B)$ for all $B\le m$. Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} then gives
$R^2(w,t)\le R^2(w,m)$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Thus an interior maximum is not evidence that predictor information ceases beyond
$B^\star$; it is a certificate of weight mismatch, and it guarantees, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:dom}, an alternative weight strictly better at the far cutoff
and no worse anywhere.

\section{Peak margins, stability, and $\pm1$ designs}

\begin{theorem}[Peak-margin identity]\label{thm:margin}
Suppose $a_i=0$ for all $t\le i<m$ and $\Sigma_t>0$. Then
\[
  R^2(w,t)-R^2(w,m)=R^2(w,t)\cdot\frac{\Sigma_m-\Sigma_t}{\Sigma_m},
\]
where $(\Sigma_m-\Sigma_t)/\Sigma_m$ is the relative added mass of the columns
admitted after $t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The numerator is frozen beyond $t$, so $R^2(w,m)=A_t^2/(\Sigma_m\|y\|^2)$ and the
difference is
$\frac{A_t^2}{\|y\|^2}\bigl(\Sigma_t^{-1}-\Sigma_m^{-1}\bigr)
= R^2(w,t)\frac{\Sigma_m-\Sigma_t}{\Sigma_m}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If the relative added mass is at most $\varepsilon$, the peak margin is at most
$R^2(w,t)\,\varepsilon$.
\end{corollary}

A near-tie between an interior peak and the far cutoff is therefore
\emph{equivalent} to the tail columns carrying little weighted mass --- precisely
the situation for a decaying weight such as $\ell^{-1/2}$ over the tail
$400<\ell\le1600$.

\begin{theorem}[Matched plateau]\label{thm:plateau}
If $a_i=0$ for all $t\le i<m$, then $R^2(w^{\mf},B)=R^2(w^{\mf},t)$ for all
$t\le B\le m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$E(B)=E(t)$ since every added term vanishes; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:mf}.
\end{proof}

So the matched filter's argmax set is the whole tail $[t,m]$ and always contains
the edge: a matched (or near-matched) weight structurally cannot exhibit a unique
interior maximum.

\begin{theorem}[Stability under small perturbations]\label{thm:stab}
Let $G$ be a finite grid, $f$ the true curve, $t\in G$, and $\delta>0$ with
$f(u)+\delta\le f(t)$ for all $u\in G\setminus\{t\}$. If
$|g(u)-f(u)|<\delta/2$ for all $u\in G$, then $g(u)<g(t)$ for all
$u\in G\setminus\{t\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$g(t)>f(t)-\delta/2\ge f(u)+\delta/2>g(u)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the $\delta/2$ threshold]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $f$ be a curve, $t\ne u_0$, $\delta>0$ with $f(t)-f(u_0)=\delta$. For every
$\varepsilon>0$ there is $g$ with $\sup_u|g(u)-f(u)|\le\delta/2+\varepsilon$ and
$g(t)<g(u_0)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Raise $f$ at $u_0$ by $\delta/2+\varepsilon$ and lower it at $t$ by the same
amount; the gap $\delta$ is overturned by $2\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

Half the top-two gap is thus exactly the noise budget of an argmax report. A
bimodal bootstrap argmax is expected whenever resampling noise is comparable to
that budget, and the honest report is then a saturation \emph{region}.

\begin{definition}[Sign design]
A \emph{sign design} of size $(n,m)$ is a family $c_0,\dots,c_{m-1}\in\{\pm1\}^n$
that is pairwise balanced, $\sum_j c_i(j)c_k(j)=0$ for $i\ne k$, together with a
response $y$ with $\|y\|^2>0$. Equivalently the columns form an orthogonal array
of strength two; Hadamard rows are the canonical example.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
Every sign design with $n>0$ is a window model, with all column masses equal to
$n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\|c_i\|^2=\sum_j c_i(j)^2=n$ since entries are $\pm1$; orthogonality is the
balance condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Order-4 Hadamard window]
Take $h_0=(1,1,1,1)$, $h_1=(1,-1,1,-1)$, $h_2=(1,1,-1,-1)$, $h_3=(1,-1,-1,1)$ as
columns and $y=h_0+h_1=(2,0,2,0)$. Then $s_i=4$, $a=(4,4,0,0)$, $\|y\|^2=8$.
Unit weights are matched on the signal block with $\rho=1$, $t=2$, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:sat} applies and the curve is $0,\tfrac12,1,\tfrac23,\tfrac12$,
with peak margin $R^2(2)-R^2(4)=\tfrac12$, matching Theorem~\ref{thm:margin}:
$1\cdot\frac{16-8}{16}=\tfrac12$.
\end{example}

\section{What the peak location does not tell you}

\begin{theorem}[Realizability of every interior location]\label{thm:real}
Let $v_0,\dots,v_{m-1}\in\R^n$ be pairwise orthogonal and nonzero and let
$1\le t<m$. Put $y=v_0+\cdots+v_{t-1}$. Then $(v,y)$ is a window model and the
unit-weight curve satisfies $R^2(\mathbf{1},B)<R^2(\mathbf{1},t)$ for all
$B\le m$, $B\ne t$; its argmax set is exactly $\{t\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By orthogonality $\ip{v_k}{y}=s_k$ for $k<t$ and $0$ otherwise, and
$\|y\|^2=\sum_{i<t}s_i>0$. With unit weights the first $t$ columns satisfy
$a_i=s_i>0$, the matched condition with $\rho=1$, while every later column is
pure noise with unit weight. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sat}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Inside a fixed sign design of strength two, every interior location
$1\le t<m$ occurs as the unique argmax for a suitable response.
\end{corollary}

Within a fixed column family the peak location can therefore be moved anywhere by
changing the response alone: a measured $B^\star$ constrains the
response--weight pair, not the columns.

\begin{theorem}[Failure of unimodality]\label{thm:bimodal}
Call $f$ \emph{unimodal} on $\{0,\dots,m\}$ if there is $t\le m$ with $f$
nondecreasing on $[0,t]$ and nonincreasing on $[t,m]$. Take $n=m=3$, columns the
orthonormal basis $e_0,e_1,e_2$ of $\R^3$, unit weights and $y=(3,1,1)$, so
$a=(3,1,1)$, $s=(1,1,1)$, $\|y\|^2=11$. Then
\[
  R^2(1)=\tfrac{27}{33},\qquad R^2(2)=\tfrac{24}{33},\qquad
  R^2(3)=\tfrac{25}{33},
\]
so the curve has a strict interior local minimum at $B=2$, hence two strict local
maxima at $B=1$ and $B=3$, and is not unimodal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$A_1=3,\Sigma_1=1$ gives $9/11=27/33$; $A_2=4,\Sigma_2=2$ gives
$16/22=24/33$; $A_3=5,\Sigma_3=3$ gives $25/33$. Unimodality would force either a
nondecreasing step at $B=1$ or a nonincreasing step at $B=2$; both fail.
\end{proof}

The columns are perfectly orthonormal, so no mass heterogeneity is responsible;
and the per-column efficiencies $a_i/s_i=3,1,1$ are already sorted in
nonincreasing order, so the bimodality is not an artefact of column ordering.
Dilution is self-limiting: the second column has efficiency $1$ against a running
slope of $3$ and dilutes badly, but it drags the slope down to $2$, so the third
column of the same efficiency is much less out of step and accumulation wins.

\begin{proposition}[Same data, matched weight]
For the model of Theorem~\ref{thm:bimodal} the matched filter is
$w^{\mf}=(3,1,1)$ and its curve is $\tfrac{9}{11},\tfrac{10}{11},1$, strictly
increasing to a perfect fit.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$E(B)=9,10,11$ for $B=1,2,3$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:mf} with $\|y\|^2=11$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Window curve evaluation.} Given masses $s_i$, signals $a_i$,
$\|y\|^2$, a weight $w$ and a grid $G$, compute prefix sums $A_B,\Sigma_B$ in one
pass and emit $A_B^2/(\Sigma_B\|y\|^2)$ for $B\in G$. Cost $O(m)$ time, $O(1)$
extra space.

\paragraph{B. Matched-filter audit.} Compute $E(B)=\sum_{i<B}a_i^2/s_i$ by prefix
sum; report the matched curve $E(B)/\|y\|^2$ (nondecreasing by
Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}) and the global cap $E(m)/\|y\|^2$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}); if the user's weight has $R^2(w,t)>R^2(w,m)$ for some
$t<m$, emit the mismatch certificate of Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}. Cost $O(m)$.

\paragraph{C. Argmax margin and stability budget.} Compute the curve on the grid,
let $\delta$ be the gap between best and second-best values, and report
$(\arg\max,\delta/2)$. By Theorems~\ref{thm:stab} and \ref{thm:sharp} this is
exactly the perturbation budget. Applied to the motivating data with
$\delta=0.0105$ the budget is $0.00525$, far below plausible resampling noise on
$n=128$ samples --- which is why the bootstrap argmax is bimodal, with mass
$276/500$ at $400$ and $178/500$ at $1600$. Cost $O(|G|\log|G|)$.

\section{Discussion}

The theory permits three precise statements in place of one imprecise one.
First, \emph{dominance is real and is the right claim}: the $\ell^{-1/2}$ weight
beating $\ell^{-1}$ at all five cutoffs, with no weight-by-cutoff interaction, is
the empirical shadow of Theorem~\ref{thm:dom}. Second, \emph{the interior peak is
a diagnosis}: by Theorem~\ref{thm:cert} its existence certifies that
$\ell^{-1/2}$ is not matched, so something strictly better exists; the correct
follow-up is to estimate $a_i/s_i$ per column and audit the matched curve against
the cap $E(m)/\|y\|^2$. Third, \emph{the location is not a measurement of
arithmetic}: by Theorem~\ref{thm:real} every interior location is realisable
inside any fixed orthogonal family, and by Theorems~\ref{thm:margin},
\ref{thm:stab}, \ref{thm:sharp} the observed $0.0105$ margin makes the location
statistically soft in a quantitatively predicted way. The recomputed
harmonic-weight plateau with a $+0.006$ edge maximum is likewise explained by
Theorem~\ref{thm:plateau}.

\paragraph{Limitations.} Exact pairwise orthogonality is assumed; real
prime-indicator columns are only approximately orthogonal. The column statistics
$(s_i,a_i)$ are treated as known, whereas in practice they are estimated --- which
is why the stability budget is part of the recommended report. Finally the
analysis concerns a single aggregated predictor; the matched filter is the best
one-dimensional compression, and Theorem~\ref{thm:cap} caps only what this
instrument can do, not what a joint multiple regression could.

\paragraph{Relation to classical ideas.} Theorem~\ref{thm:dom} is a
Cauchy--Schwarz statement and $a_i/s_i$ is the matched filter of detection
theory, recovered here as the pointwise maximiser over the entire cutoff grid at
once. What is distinctive is the inversion: the monotonicity of the matched curve
is used as a certificate, so an observed interior peak becomes a proof of
mismatch, and the realizability and non-unimodality results strip the peak
location of the structural meaning experimenters routinely assign to it.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Correlated-column window calculus.} If the Gram matrix $G$ of the
window columns satisfies $\|G-D\|\le\varepsilon\lambda_{\min}(D)$ for its
diagonal part $D$, conjecturally dilution, matched dominance and the peak-margin
identity all hold up to a multiplicative $1+O(\varepsilon)$: every proof passes
through the two scalars $A=\ip{S}{y}$ and $\Sigma=\|S\|^2$, and a Gram
perturbation perturbs only $\Sigma$, one-sidedly.

\item \textbf{Quantitative converse to the certificate.} Conjecture: if the peak
at $t$ exceeds the edge value by $\delta$, the weight lies at angular distance at
least $c\delta$ from the matched direction in the $s$-weighted inner product,
with $c$ depending only on $R^2(t)$. The peak-margin identity turns the drop into
a relative mass and Cauchy--Schwarz turns that mass into an angle; the missing
step is the equality case.

\item \textbf{How many local maxima can a window curve have?} Conjecture: with
$m$ columns of equal mass and nonincreasing signals $a_1\ge\cdots\ge a_m\ge0$,
the number of strict local maxima of $k\mapsto A_k^2/k$ is at most
$\lfloor\log_2 m\rfloor+1$, sharp for a geometric signal profile.

\item \textbf{Prescribed local-maximum sets.} Which \emph{sets} of local maxima
are realisable inside a fixed orthogonal family? Theorem~\ref{thm:real} settles
the single-location case and Theorem~\ref{thm:bimodal} is the base case of a
general construction.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Window curves are among the most common diagnostic plots in applied quantitative
work, and the location of their maximum is routinely reported as a substantive
finding. For orthogonal predictor families that reading is unsupported. The
rise-then-fall shape is real, but the fall is dilution by mass, not exhaustion of
information; the peak location is realisable at any interior cutoff within any
fixed column family; the curve need not even be unimodal; and an interior peak is
a certificate that the weight in use is not matched, so a strictly better weight
exists dominating it at every cutoff simultaneously. What survives, and is worth
reporting, is the pair (matched-filter curve, global cap $E(m)/\|y\|^2$) together
with a stability budget equal to half the top-two gap --- all computable in a
single linear pass.

